  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "M.K.Thomas vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Monday, The 18Th Day Of January 2016/28Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 3178 of 2010 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 401/2008 Of The Additional Sessions Court, Fast Track-Ii, Thiruvananthapuram Cc 124/2001 Of The Judicial First Class Magistrate Court-Ii, Thiruvananthapuram Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Ist Accused: ------------------------------------------ M.K.Thomas, La-Delicous Pastryshop, Opp.Trivandrum Tennis Club, Thiruvananthapuram. By Advs.Sri.P.Vijaya Bhanu (Sr.) Sri.P.M.Rafiq Sri.V.C.Sarath Respondents/Complainant & State: -------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Corporation Of Thiruvananthapuram. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. By Public Prosecutor Sri.V.S.Sreejith M.K.Thomas vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/52107535/ 1 This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 18-01-2016 , The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P. No.3178 of 2010 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 18th day of January, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 17.10.2000, PW1, the Food Inspector purchased three packets of banana chips, each packet having a weight of 200 gram each, from the first accused. The second accused was the licencee of the said shop. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. The Public Analyst submitted Ext.P13 report stating that the sample contained synthetic food colour tartrazine and was therefore adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 and PW2 were examined and Exts.P1 to P20 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 was examined and Exts.D1 to D3 were marked for the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P13 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P13 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) M.K.Thomas vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/52107535/ 2 the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P13 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P13 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) M.K.Thomas vs Food Inspector on 18 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/52107535/ 2 the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
